I am trying to use an openmp for loop if a certain condition holds. I could simply use an if else statement to use the parallel for loop if a condition holds, but the code in the for loop is a bit long and it would double the length of the code if I just use the if else statement. So basically, I want a better way to do this: if(condition_holds){
   // use parallel for loop
   #pragma omp parallel for
   for(...){
     // Long piece of code
   }
}else{
  // Don't use parallel for loop
  for(...){
    // Long piece of code
  }
} so I won't have to write the code inside the for loop twice.

answers.body:
Use OpenMP's if clause to conditionally enable parallelism: #pragma omp parallel for if(condition_holds)
for(...) {

} You will probably get an overhead of one additional function call because the loop body is separated into a function by the OpenMP implementation.

