I'm trying to parallelize some IIR filtering code, so far using gcc 7.2 and gomp . Here's the relevant section of code: void par_iir(std::vector<std::vector<double>>& data, const std::vector<double>& B, int order) {
    int N = data[0].size();
    const int CHUNK_SIZE = 32768;
    int chunks = ceil((double)(N - 2) / (double)CHUNK_SIZE);
    int gChunk;

    #pragma omp parallel private(gChunk) shared(chunks, order, B, data)
    {
        for (gChunk = 0; gChunk < chunks + order - 1; gChunk++) {
            #pragma omp for
            for (int i = 0; i < order; i++) {
                auto chunk = gChunk - i;

                if (chunk >= 0 && chunk < chunks) {
                    auto sz = chunk == (chunks - 1) ? (N - 2) % CHUNK_SIZE : CHUNK_SIZE;
                    auto inp = data[i].data();
                    auto out = data[i + 1].data();

                    filter(inp + chunk * CHUNK_SIZE + 2, B.data() + i * 5, out + chunk * CHUNK_SIZE + 2, sz);
                }
            }
        }
    }
} The function filter is defined as follows: inline void filter(const double* in, const double* B, double* out, int sz) {
    for (int i = 0; i < sz; ++i)
        out[i] = in[i] * B[0] + in[i - 1] * B[1] + in[i - 2] * B[2] - out[i - 1] * B[3] - out[i- 2] * B[4];
} par_iir defines a cascaded IIR filter and filter applies a single bi-quad in the cascade. order determines how many cascaded bi-quads make up the filter. My computer has 4 cores (8 threads) and I've chosen a filter with order 8. Since IIR filters have loop dependencies, I've opted to divide the work into chunks (currently of a fixed size of 32768.) The idea is that once a chunk for bi-quad i in the cascade is completed, then bi-quad i and i+1 can run in parallel to completion. I was expecting that this should result in close to all 8 threads being maximally used, so an improvement of close to 8x. Instead, I typically see about 33% load on each thread. Using perf I've profiled the code and found that ~63% of the time is spent in gomp_thread_start . This ~63% doesn't change even if I increase N and CHUNK_SIZE proportionally. Where is this 63% overhead coming from? And secondarily, is there a better way to write this code? Some details on IIR filters in case it helps: For my purposes, an IIR filter is made of up multiple bi-quad filters. Each bi-quad filter performs the following operation, where the a and b values are constants: out[n] = b0*in[n] + b1*in[n-1] + b2*in[n-2] - a1*out[n-1] - a2*out[n-2] Each section of the cascade is given the output of the prior section as its input. This makes most approaches to parallelism unworkable.

