answers.body:
If you use only OpenMP for parallelism, and you have nested parallelism enabled you could use the num_threads argument to the pragma omp parallel to specify to use half of the threads: int const currentNumThreads = omp_get_num_threads();
int const maxNumThreads = omp_get_max_num_threads();
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(maxNumThreads/currentNumThreads)
for ( ... ) {
   ...
} Even if you are using mixed threading technologies (which seems to be your case), you could still accomplish the same by using different methods to set currentNumThreads and maxNumThreads . A word of caution though. Using nested parallelism is generally inadvisable for OpenMP, as it makes code quite fragile and rigid . Any change to your run() function needs to be aware of where it is called from, and any future calls to run() need to be aware of whats inside of it. Both in terms of performance and maintenance, trying to follow a data parallel approach for OpenMP usage is best. That is, perform a similar set of operations in each thread, but on a different chunk of data. The exception to this, is if you are using OpenMP tasks, which you can spawn, and let the scheduler take care of it. Using task parallelism in a parallel for loop will tend to lead to poor performance due to a lack of data locality and high overhead if the tasks are small.

