You have many race conditions on all matrix accesses on j . For example MatJIplus  ( 0, j ) = ... will be set concurrently by different iterations of the outer loop. Since these data structures are indirect, you cannot just declare them as private, you must construct them in the worker threads themselves. Since you only use the parts of the matrices that are written within each loop body, you could declare them inside the loop body. However, to avoid reallocating those again and again in every loop, you can split the parallel for as such: arma::mat MatIplusI ( 3 , iS ) ;
#pragma omp parallel
{
    // Matrices will be implicitly private and correctly constructed locally for each thread.
    arma::mat MatJplusIplus ( 3 , iS ) ;
    arma::mat MatJI ( 3 , iS ) ;
    arma::mat MatJplusI ( 3 , iS ) ;
    arma::mat MatJIplus ( 3 , iS ) ;
    arma::mat MatJplusJ ( 3 , iS ) ;
    #pragma omp parallel for private(j) reduction(+: Wr) private(Omega)
    {
        for( i = 0 ; i < iS- (3) ; i++ ) Note that reduction on Omega makes absolutely no sense, it should be private instead. Also it makes little sense to allocate memory for the full matrices, when you only use columns of them within each iteration. Just make a suitable private datastructure to hold the column(i/j) contents in the first place.

